WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.259
So now we know the genetic makeup of an AI agent and how we can achieve navigation.

2
00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:10.259
We can now look at how we can allow our AI agents to query the world for information,

3
00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:13.259
and this is where the Environment Query System really comes in.

4
00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:18.260
The Environment Query System is a feature within Unreal that is used to gather data about the environment.

5
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.649
Example use of this can be to find an ideal position to attack the player from,

6
00:00:22.649 --> 00:00:26.190
or to find where the nearest health pickup might be.

7
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:32.609
It's important to note that the Environment Query System plugin must be enabled in order to access this in the editor.

8
00:00:32.609 --> 00:00:38.609
Environment Queries are a powerful tool for developers, each query is tailored to the scenario in mind.

9
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:44.859
An Environment Query is made up of generators, tests and contexts to provide a full desired result,

10
00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:47.859
and we'll discuss each one of those in just a second.

11
00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:51.280
Environment Queries can be called from within the Behaviour Tree directly,

12
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.280
but they can also be called in isolation from the native C++.

13
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.020
Although we are discussing Environment Queries in the context of AI,

14
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.019
it's important to note that we can make use of this system to drive pretty much any other gameplay system.

15
00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.719
In the image on the left we can see that we create an Environment Query

16
00:01:07.719 --> 00:01:11.879
much in the same way that we create a Behaviour Tree or a Blackboard Asset.

17
00:01:11.879 --> 00:01:15.879
The image to the right however shows what an Environment Query looks like in the editor content browser.

18
00:01:15.879 --> 00:01:19.810
Let's take a look at how we go about creating our own Environment Queries,

19
00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.810
and it's only natural that we start with the generators.

20
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.290
Generators are key to the genetic makeup of an EQS.

21
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.319
They are essentially used to generate an array of locations or actors.

22
00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:36.379
These are often referred to as items, and these items can then be tested and weighed.

23
00:01:36.379 --> 00:01:39.450
There are different types of generator already in Unreal,

24
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:44.450
so a small example of these would be generating a list of all actors of class to return a list of actors.

25
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:49.450
Alternatively, we can generate a list of points in a circle, grid or donor orientation.

26
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.989
So now that we know that a generator is used to give us a set of actors or locations known as items.

27
00:01:54.989 --> 00:02:00.379
However, a generator alone is not capable of doing such a task without the use of contexts.

28
00:02:00.379 --> 00:02:05.500
Environment contexts give generators and tests a frame of reference to use.

29
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.729
Contexts can range from something simple such as the querier,

30
00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.729
to more complex contexts such as all actors of class.

31
00:02:12.729 --> 00:02:16.979
Contexts can be set up to return one or many items.

32
00:02:16.979 --> 00:02:23.530
It's important to note that whatever generator you choose may not be fully compatible with the intended context.

33
00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:29.530
More specifically, not all generators can produce items based off of an input of multiple contexts.

34
00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.199
On top of all the contexts that come out of the box with Unreal,

35
00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:37.199
we can also create our own context in both Blueprint and C++.

36
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:43.740
So now we know we can use a generator to give us a set of actors or locations known as items.

37
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:50.030
However, a generator alone is not capable of doing such a task without the use of contexts.

38
00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.030
Environment contexts give generators and tests a frame of reference to use.

39
00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.639
Contexts can range from something as simple as the querier,

40
00:02:57.639 --> 00:03:01.639
to more complex contexts such as all actors of class.

41
00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:05.639
Contexts can be set up to return one or many items,

42
00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:09.639
but it's important to know what generator you're going to be pairing with what context.

43
00:03:09.639 --> 00:03:13.639
Not all generators can deal with multiple items being returned by a context.

44
00:03:13.639 --> 00:03:17.960
On top of contexts already provided by the Unreal Engine,

45
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.960
you can also create your own contexts both in Blueprint and in C++.

46
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.060
So let's quickly recap what we know so far.

47
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.060
We can use generators to create a choice of items,

48
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.060
and contexts give us a reference to use when generating those items.

49
00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.060
So whilst we can use these two in conjunction to give a result,

50
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.060
we can make use of tests to better refine our result.

51
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:43.379
Tests can be performed to determine which item is the best option out of all of them.

52
00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:47.379
Whatever best means in any given circumstance can be tweaked depending on the developer

53
00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:49.379
and the technical specification of the task.

54
00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:52.979
Unreal already offers a wide range of tests that we can make use of,

55
00:03:52.979 --> 00:03:56.979
so a small example of these would be sorting all items by distance,

56
00:03:56.979 --> 00:04:00.979
sorting all items by the dot product in relation to the querier's orientation,

57
00:04:00.979 --> 00:04:05.340
filtering out items that don't have line of sight with the querier,

58
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.659
and then we can see if these items are overlapping with anything in the world.

59
00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:13.659
So for instance, are these items generated within the bounds of some kind of geometry?

60
00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:18.579
However, like we said, if these tests don't meet your particular specifications,

61
00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:20.579
then by all means you can author your own tests,

62
00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:24.189
and these can be created quite easily within C++.

63
00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:27.189
Multiple tests can be attached to a single generator

64
00:04:27.189 --> 00:04:30.189
so we can mix and match to get the best results possible.

65
00:04:30.189 --> 00:04:32.740
As we can see in the above image,

66
00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.740
there are some common properties of the tests that we can actually discuss.

67
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.089
The test purpose defines what our test aims to do,

68
00:04:39.089 --> 00:04:43.089
whether that is to be filter them out from the entire batch entirely,

69
00:04:43.089 --> 00:04:46.089
to score the items, or a combination of the two.

70
00:04:46.089 --> 00:04:49.149
It's important to know that in the case of filter and score,

71
00:04:49.149 --> 00:04:53.149
filtering will be done before scoring to avoid calculating the score on items

72
00:04:53.149 --> 00:04:55.149
that have already been filtered out.

73
00:04:55.149 --> 00:04:58.730
Each test will expose a different set of filter properties

74
00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.730
if filtering or filter and score is selected in the test purpose dropdown.

75
00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.759
In the example above, we've used the distance test,

76
00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:08.759
which will filter out any items that are further away than our desired threshold.

77
00:05:08.759 --> 00:05:12.399
With scoring, we follow a similar pattern.

78
00:05:12.399 --> 00:05:15.399
If score or score and filter are selected in the test purpose,

79
00:05:15.399 --> 00:05:18.399
then we get to decide how our items will be scored.

80
00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:24.040
Again, the score properties will vary depending on the test that's being used.

81
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.040
In the example above, we can define that points further away from the querier,

82
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.040
our AI agent in this case,

83
00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.199
will be weighted heavier than those that are closer to the querier.

84
00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:36.199
However, if we wish to flip this around, we could do this in two ways.

85
00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:41.199
The first way would be to flip the scoring equation to use inverse linear.

86
00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:45.199
The second way in which we could achieve this is by altering the scoring factor to be negative one.

87
00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:48.199
We're essentially going to flip the return value.

88
00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:52.870
It's highly recommended that you place filtering tests ahead of your scoring tests,

89
00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:56.870
again so the scoring calculations can only take place on valid items.
